      summary: "The Pioneer New England Legal Foundation, which represents the owner of 148 State St. in a class action against the City of Boston, said on August 18, 2026 that data the city provided shows assessments rose on 150 commercial properties, in 182 instances, after owners appealed to the state Appellate Tax Board, Banker & Tradesman reported. The foundation puts the disputed payments at about $19 million. Suffolk Superior Court Judge Peter B. Krupp let the case proceed in May, writing that the alleged Add-Back Policy \"plausibly chills all commercial property owners from exercising their right to seek an abatement.\" City officials have said they \"stabilized\" the values of properties with open appeals at prior-year levels.",
