      id: "live-108",
      seoTitle: "Hexagon US Federal Lease Renewal Upheld; $174,987.45 Fee Reversed",
      seoDescription: "Alabama Supreme Court upheld Hexagon US Federal's lease renewal against landlord CBS Holdings but reversed a $174,987.45 fee award over redacted bills.",
      addedDate: "2026-09-10",
      title: "CBS Holdings, LLC v. Hexagon US Federal, Inc. (Madison County Lease Renewal & Fee Fight)",
      brief: "After Hexagon US Federal won its lease-renewal case against new owner CBS Holdings, the Alabama Supreme Court found heavily redacted bills did not support its $174,987.45 fee award and sent it back.",
      category: "lease-disputes",
      status: "appeal",
      date: "2025-07-11",
      jurisdiction: "Supreme Court of Alabama",
      parties: [
        { name: "CBS Holdings, LLC", role: "Appellant" },
        { name: "Hexagon US Federal, Inc.", role: "Appellee" }
      ],
      amountUsd: 174987.45,
      amountBasis: "award",
      judge: "Gregory Cook",
      state: "AL",
      amount: "$174,987.45 in disputed attorney fees; underlying lease renewal upheld",
      source: "live",
      sourceUrl: "https://law.justia.com/cases/alabama/supreme-court/2024/sc-2024-0077.html",
      documentUrl: "https://caselaw.findlaw.com/court/al-supreme-court/117470240.html",
      documentLabel: "Read the attorney-fee opinion",
      summary: "Hexagon US Federal, Inc. ('HexFed') sued CBS Holdings, LLC in Madison Circuit Court in April 2021 after CBS Holdings, which bought the Huntsville building in 2016, rejected HexFed's notice renewing its lease of two bays. After a bench trial, the court ruled for HexFed on all claims and on CBS Holdings' counterclaims, reformed the lease to correct a mutual mistake and held HexFed was entitled to fees; the Alabama Supreme Court affirmed on October 18, 2024. In a second appeal decided July 11, 2025, the court reversed the $174,987.45 award of costs and fees because HexFed's invoices were almost entirely redacted, and remanded for HexFed to support its application and for the trial court to explain its calculation.",
      significance: "In Alabama, winning a contractual fee award in a commercial lease case does not end the fee fight. An application based on time spent must describe the work well enough for the court to check it, and the order must show how the court weighed the Peebles factors. HexFed keeps its entitlement to fees but must document the amount on remand.",
      body: [
        "Hexagon US Federal, Inc. (HexFed) leased two bays in a building on Dunlop Boulevard in Huntsville from a related company, Intergraph Unimproved Properties, LLC, in 2015. A 2016 amended lease put both bays on a five-year term but stated the maximum rent for one bay only for 12 months. CBS Holdings, LLC bought the building in November 2016 and became the landlord. In July 2019 CBS Holdings first asserted that the bay's term had been 12 months; HexFed disputed that and stayed at the lease rate. In December 2020 HexFed sent notice renewing the lease, and CBS Holdings rejected it, citing among other reasons that the notice misstated the renewal rents. HexFed sued in April 2021 for a declaration that it had renewed and for breach of the covenant of quiet enjoyment. CBS Holdings counterclaimed for breach of contract, ejectment and unjust enrichment and sought a landlord's lien.",
        "Following a December 18, 2023 bench trial, the Madison Circuit Court ruled for HexFed on all claims and counterclaims. It found CBS Holdings had waived its 12-month argument by accepting rent without objection and by signing a 2020 lease amendment without changing the term, reformed the lease to correct the parties' mutual mistake on the maximum rent, declared that HexFed had properly renewed, and awarded HexFed costs and attorneys' fees in an amount to be set later. The Alabama Supreme Court affirmed on October 18, 2024 (No. SC-2024-0077), holding among other things that the miscalculated rent in the renewal notice did not void a renewal sent with the required 90 days' written notice. It overruled CBS Holdings' application for rehearing on December 20, 2024.",
        "On April 16, 2024, after a hearing, the trial court awarded HexFed $174,987.45 in costs and attorneys' fees in a short order. CBS Holdings appealed, arguing that HexFed's invoices, with 449 of 458 charges fully redacted on privilege grounds, did not let the court judge whether the work was reasonable and necessary. On July 11, 2025 (No. SC-2024-0308), the Alabama Supreme Court agreed in an opinion by Justice Cook, holding that a fee application based on time spent must describe the billed work well enough for the court to calculate a fee, and that the order must show how the court calculated the award and considered the Peebles factors. It reversed and remanded with instructions. Justice Sellers, joined by Justices Wise and Bryan, concurred in the result.",
        "The lease dispute itself was resolved in HexFed's favor, with the trial court declaring HexFed entitled to occupy the space through at least March 31, 2026. What remained on remand was the amount of costs and fees CBS Holdings must pay."
      ],
      timeline: [
        { when: "November 14, 2016", label: "CBS Holdings buys the building and takes over the lease" },
        { when: "April 21, 2021", label: "HexFed sues CBS Holdings in Madison Circuit Court over the lease renewal" },
        { when: "January 12, 2024", label: "Trial court rules for HexFed on all claims and reforms the lease" },
        { when: "April 16, 2024", label: "Trial court awards HexFed $174,987.45 in costs and attorney fees" },
        { when: "October 18, 2024", label: "Alabama Supreme Court affirms the merits judgment for HexFed (SC-2024-0077); rehearing overruled December 20, 2024" },
        { when: "July 11, 2025", label: "Alabama Supreme Court reverses the fee award over inadequate billing support and remands (SC-2024-0308)", current: true }
      ],
