      documentUrl: "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/10627629/the-state-of-mississippi-and-the-secretary-of-state-of-the-state-of/",
      documentLabel: "Read the Mississippi Supreme Court's opinion",
      summary: "On April 4, 2024, the Mississippi Supreme Court affirmed a Harrison County Chancery Court ruling that roughly one acre of waterfront land in East Biloxi belongs to the family that has long held it, not to the State. The State and the Secretary of State had claimed the land as public trust tidelands under a 1994 boundary map; the chancellor traced the family's title to a 1784 Spanish land grant. The family once ran the Fisherman's Wharf restaurant on the site, and the land was later leased for the Lady Luck Casino, the Sun Herald reported.",
      significance: "The ruling confirms private title to a waterfront tract that the owner has optioned for a proposed casino development, with the City of Biloxi and Harrison County siding with the owner against the State. It also shows that the Secretary of State's 1994 tidelands map can be overcome where the State did not follow the statutory mapping procedures and the owner's title traces to a pre-statehood grant.",
      body: [
        "The dispute began with a map the Mississippi Secretary of State published in 1994 to mark the line between private property and State-owned public trust tidelands along the coast. The map put a waterfront parcel south of U.S. 90 and west of Oak Street in Biloxi on the State's side of the line. The family that owns the land challenged the boundary in Harrison County Chancery Court in 1998, and the State countered that the land was artificial fill rather than part of the family's property, WLOX reported. The Supreme Court's opinion and the Sun Herald describe the parcel as about one acre; WLOX described it as 1.5 acres.",
        "In 2022 the chancellor ruled for the family. As WLOX summarized the ruling, it rested on a 1784 Spanish land grant at the root of the family's title, on the State's failure to follow the statutory procedures for drawing its map, and on insufficient proof that the fill was not oyster shells and dredge spoils deposited by outside parties. The State and Secretary of State Michael Watson appealed.",
        "The Supreme Court affirmed on April 4, 2024, in an opinion by Justice David Ishee. The Sun Herald reported that the court found the family's title traces to the Spanish grant and held that filled land built for \"a higher public purpose\" belongs to the upland owner. The City of Biloxi and Harrison County sided with the family. The Secretary of State's office told the Sun Herald it was still reviewing the opinion.",
        "The family once ran the Fisherman's Wharf restaurant on the site, and the land was leased for the Lady Luck Casino, which opened onshore in 1992 and closed in 1998, according to the Sun Herald. WLOX reported that the property is part of the planned Tullis Gardens Hotel & Casino project, whose developers were waiting on the ruling before applying for site approval, and that a pending bill, Senate Bill 2780, would bar the previously approved gaming site from being used for a casino again. The owner told WLOX the litigation had kept him from selling or leasing the land, and Harrison County's attorney said the decision matters for coastal waterfront owners, especially those whose titles trace to Spanish land grants."
      ],
