      id: "live-147",
      seoTitle: "Circle K Parking-Lot Fall: Fourth Circuit Revives Premises Suit",
      seoDescription: "Fourth Circuit revived a customer's fall suit against Circle K over a lot washed with a chemical cleaner; the case was later dismissed on a settlement order.",
      addedDate: "2026-09-16",
      title: "Circle K Stores Parking-Lot Fall Appeal (Fourth Circuit Premises-Liability Summary-Judgment Reversal)",
      brief: "The Fourth Circuit revived a customer's fall suit against Circle K, holding a jury must decide whether a lot just washed with a chemical cleaner was an open and obvious hazard. The case was dismissed on a settlement order before trial.",
      category: "premises-liability",
      status: "settled",
      date: "2026-05-13",
      jurisdiction: "U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fourth Circuit",
      parties: [
        { name: "Circle K Stores Inc.", role: "Defendant-Appellee" }
      ],
      amountUsd: null,
      state: "SC",
      amount: "Settlement terms not disclosed; the Fourth Circuit said the customer's medical expenses were about $430,000",
      source: "live",
      sourceUrl: "https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca4/25-1964/25-1964-2026-05-13.html",
      summary: "On May 13, 2026, the Fourth Circuit reversed summary judgment for Circle K Stores Inc. in a suit by a customer who fell in the parking lot of a Myrtle Beach, South Carolina store after an employee washed a painted line with water and a powdered concrete cleaner. Judge J. Harvie Wilkinson III, joined by Judges Julius N. Richardson and Toby Heytens, held that whether the hazard was 'open and obvious' was a question for a jury and that expert testimony was not required to prove causation. On remand, the district court dismissed the case under a 60-day settlement order on October 26, 2026, the day before trial was to begin.",
      significance: "The opinion narrows the open-and-obvious defense that property owners and retail operators often use to win premises cases at summary judgment: in the court's view, visible wetness alone does not make a surface obviously dangerous when the real hazard is an unannounced cleaning chemical. The absence of signs, cones or the safety vest that company policy appeared to require weighed against the store.",
      body: [
        "On May 13, 2026, the U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fourth Circuit reversed a grant of summary judgment to Circle K Stores Inc. in a premises-liability suit by a customer who fell in the parking lot of a Circle K in Myrtle Beach, South Carolina. In a published opinion by Judge J. Harvie Wilkinson III, joined by Judges Julius N. Richardson and Toby Heytens, the court held that a jury, not a judge, must decide whether the hazard was 'open and obvious,' and that the customer did not need expert testimony to connect his fall to his injury.",
        "According to the opinion, on October 19, 2022, a store employee was sweeping outside the entrance with water and a powdered concrete cleaner. He was not wearing the yellow reflective vest that company policy required, and no signs or cones warned that cleaning was under way. The customer, leaving after paying for gas, stepped on a wet painted line, slipped and ruptured his patellar tendon; the court said he incurred about $430,000 in medical expenses. He sued in state court in November 2022, and Circle K removed the case to the U.S. District Court for the District of South Carolina, which granted summary judgment on three grounds: the wet surface was open and obvious, Circle K could not have foreseen the specific danger, and there was no expert testimony on causation.",
        "Circle K defended two of those grounds on appeal, and the Fourth Circuit rejected both. It reasoned that visible wetness on concrete does not by itself make a surface an obvious danger, because the real hazard was the unannounced use of a chemical cleaner that a customer could not reliably detect by sight. To win summary judgment, the court said, a store owner must show either that the hazard was so obvious no reasonable invitee would encounter it or that it took sufficient steps to flag the danger, and Circle K showed neither. On causation, the court held that a slip-and-fall is within jurors' ordinary experience, although either side may offer experts, including on the customer's earlier tendon injury. The court also vacated the district court's rulings on the customer's Daubert motion and on his spoliation motion, which concerned a contractor's restriping of the lot before his expert could inspect it.",
        "On remand, the district court ruled on pretrial matters in October 2026, granting the spoliation motion in part, and a jury was selected on October 21 for a trial set to begin October 27. On October 26, the court cancelled the trial and dismissed the case under a 60-day settlement order, without prejudice to reopening if the settlement is not completed, according to the docket on CourtListener. The docket entries do not disclose the settlement terms."
      ],
      timeline: [
        { when: "October 19, 2022", label: "A customer slips on a freshly washed painted line in a Myrtle Beach Circle K parking lot" },
        { when: "November 2022", label: "The customer sues Circle K Stores Inc. in South Carolina state court" },
        { when: "April 26, 2023", label: "Circle K removes the case to the U.S. District Court for the District of South Carolina" },
        { when: "March 19, 2026", label: "Fourth Circuit hears argument" },
        { when: "May 13, 2026", label: "Fourth Circuit reverses summary judgment for Circle K, vacates the Daubert and spoliation rulings and remands" },
        { when: "October 21, 2026", label: "Jury selected for a trial set to begin October 27" },
        { when: "October 26, 2026", label: "District court cancels the trial and dismisses the case under a 60-day settlement order", current: true }
      ],
      judge: "J. Harvie Wilkinson III",
      tags: ["premises liability", "slip and fall", "fourth circuit", "south carolina", "summary judgment", "settlement"],
      docketUrl: "https://www.courtlistener.com/docket/67279084/lewis-v-circle-k-stores-inc/",
