      id: "live-197",
      seoTitle: "Bradley County, TN Data Center Zoning Ordinance Suit by SDCL",
      seoDescription: "SDCL Tennessee Propco sued Bradley County, Tennessee to void data center zoning limits on 306 acres it bought in Hiwassee River Industrial Park.",
      property: {"name": "Hiwassee River Industrial Park", "state": "TN"},
      addedDate: "2026-09-28",
      title: "SDCL Tennessee Propco LLC v. Bradley County (Data Center Zoning Ordinance Challenge)",
      brief: "Data center developer SDCL Tennessee Propco sued Bradley County, Tennessee to void rules confining data centers to one industrial district with a 3,000-foot setback, alleging skipped procedures.",
      category: "zoning-land-use",
      status: "filed",
      date: "2026-09-17",
      jurisdiction: "Bradley County Chancery Court, Tennessee",
      state: "TN",
      amount: "$19.9M land purchase underlying a challenge to the county's data-center zoning ordinance (no damages sought)",
      source: "live",
      sourceUrl: "https://www.timesfreepress.com/news/2026/sep/21/global-firm-sues-bradley-county-to-build-300-acre/",
      summary: "SDCL Tennessee Propco LLC, the developer of a planned data center campus called Project MidSpan, sued Bradley County, Tennessee in Chancery Court on Sept. 17, 2026, WTVC NewsChannel 9 reported. It asks a judge to declare invalid the county commission's April 2026 rules limiting data centers to the I-2 Special Impact Industrial District and barring them within 3,000 feet of an existing home, school or church, and to bar their enforcement against its roughly 306 acres in the Hiwassee River Industrial Park. The company, which says it paid $19,904,950 for the land, alleges the county did not follow required procedures and that the rules are arbitrary and discriminatory; Mayor Gary Davis said the county will defend them.",
      significance: "The case tests whether a county's data center restrictions can be undone for defects in how they were adopted, including planning-commission review, public notice and open-meetings requirements. It also raises whether a single eligible district combined with a 3,000-foot setback effectively excludes data centers from the unincorporated county.",
      body: [
        "SDCL Tennessee Propco LLC, a Delaware company with its principal place of business in New York, sued Bradley County in Chancery Court on Sept. 17, 2026, WTVC NewsChannel 9 reported after obtaining the complaint. The Chattanooga Times Free Press described the developer, SDCL, as a London-based firm with U.S. headquarters in New York City and reported that Bradley County Mayor Gary Davis shared the suit on Monday, Sept. 21. The project, Project MidSpan, is a planned data center campus on about 306 acres behind Walker Valley High School, in the Hiwassee River Industrial Park near Exit 33.",
        "According to WTVC's account of the complaint, SDCL took an option on the land in July 2024 and bought it on April 28, 2025 for $19,904,950. At the time, all but about 2.5 acres were zoned I-1 General Industrial, which the company says permitted a large-scale data center; the suit includes a September 2022 letter from the county's planning director saying the district was appropriate for one. On April 20, 2026, commissioners voted 14-0 to allow data centers only in the I-2 Special Impact Industrial District and to bar them within 3,000 feet of an existing home, school or church. Davis said the project's nearest proposed building would be about 1,700 feet from the high school.",
        "SDCL argues that the version commissioners approved was materially different from the one the county's regional planning commission considered and should have gone back to that commission, that the county did not give proper public notice of the final version, and that taking up the substitute without listing it on the April 20 agenda violated Tennessee's Open Meetings Act. It also claims the rules are arbitrary and discriminatory, violate equal protection and due process, and effectively exclude data centers from all of unincorporated Bradley County. It seeks a declaration that the rules are invalid, an order barring their enforcement against its property, and legal costs and attorneys' fees.",
        "Bradley County had not yet filed a response when WTVC reported. Davis said the county will defend the regulations: \"Bradley County will defend its position and the regulations adopted by your county commission.\""
      ],
      judge: null,
      parties: [
        { name: "SDCL Tennessee Propco LLC", role: "Plaintiff" },
        { name: "Bradley County, Tennessee", role: "Defendant" }
      ],
