      seoDescription: "NY's First Department affirms liability but vacates a $1.4M mechanic's-lien judgment against a Chelsea developer, remitting the case to recalculate damages.",
      addedDate: "2026-10-16",
      title: "Waterworks Operating Co., LLC v. 514 W. 24th Owner LLC (Liability Affirmed; Mechanic's-Lien Judgment Vacated for New Damages Calculation)",
      brief: "New York's First Department upheld liability but vacated a $1,396,058.74 mechanic's-lien judgment for supplier Waterworks against a Chelsea project owner, ordering damages recalculated.",
      category: "construction-defect",
      status: "ruling",
      date: "2026-10-15",
      jurisdiction: "Appellate Division of the Supreme Court of the State of New York, First Department",
      state: "NY",
      amount: "$1,396,058.74 judgment vacated and remitted for recalculation of damages",
      source: "live",
      sourceUrl: "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/11035230/waterworks-operating-co-llc-v-514-w-24th-owner-llc/",
      summary: "On October 15, 2026, New York's Appellate Division, First Department, upheld summary judgment on liability but vacated a $1,396,058.74 judgment that bathroom-fixtures supplier Waterworks Operating Company, LLC won against 514 West 24th Owner LLC over materials delivered for the owner's redevelopment of a Chelsea building. The court held the owner ratified disputed change orders by accepting the materials without objection, but found Waterworks' initial motion proved delivery of only $2,340,098 of the $2,577,166.99 contract, because its proof for the remaining $237,069 came in a reply affidavit. It remitted the case to Supreme Court on damages only.",
      significance: "The decision shows a New York court finding implied ratification of change orders when an owner accepts the delivered goods without objection, even if its construction manager lacked written authority to approve them. It also shows that proof first offered in reply papers will be disregarded on summary judgment, which here cost the supplier its judgment pending a new damages determination.",
      body: [
        "Waterworks, which makes and supplies high-end bathroom fixtures, sued 514 West 24th Owner LLC and its surety in New York County Supreme Court for breach of contract, lien foreclosure and account stated over bathroom and cabinet materials delivered for the owner's redevelopment of a building in Chelsea. Under purchase orders and change orders, the owner agreed to pay $2,577,166.99. Both sides agree the owner paid $1,608,827.37 for goods delivered and accepted, and Waterworks filed a mechanic's lien for $968,339.62, the balance it says is due.",
        "Justice Joel M. Cohen granted Waterworks summary judgment on the contract and lien claims in an order entered June 12, 2024, dismissed the account stated claim as duplicative, and on December 5, 2024, entered judgment for $1,396,058.74, based on the unpaid balance of the full contract price plus pre-judgment interest, costs and disbursements.",
        "On appeal, the owner argued it was not bound by $390,605.51 in change orders approved by the project's original construction manager, Sunrise Construction, LLC, because their agreement barred Sunrise from entering change orders without the owner's prior written approval. The panel of Justices Webber, Friedman, Kapnick and Pitt-Burke unanimously rejected that argument, holding that the owner's acceptance of the materials without objection was an implied ratification.",
        "The owner also argued there were factual disputes over how much was delivered. The court held Waterworks' invoices established delivery of $2,340,098 in materials, since the owner identified no invoiced item it did not receive. But Waterworks offered its proof that the remaining $237,069 was delivered only in an affidavit from its accounts receivable manager filed with its reply papers, which the court disregarded because the owner had no chance to respond. Because the judgment rested on the full contract price, the court vacated it and remitted the case for further proceedings on damages, and dismissed the separate appeal from the June 2024 order as subsumed."
      ],
