      id: "live-329",
      seoTitle: "Oak Harbor Home2 Suites: Park-Land Ordinance Needs SEPA Review",
      seoDescription: "Washington appeals court holds Oak Harbor's ordinance easing sale of Hal Ramaley Park land for a 107-room Home2 Suites hotel needs SEPA review.",
      property: {"name": "Proposed Home2 Suites by Hilton (adjacent to Hal Ramaley Memorial Park)", "address": null, "city": "Oak Harbor", "state": "WA"},
      addedDate: "2026-10-23",
      title: "Whidbey Environmental Action Network v. City of Oak Harbor: Washington Appeals Court Revives SEPA Challenge to Park-Land Ordinance Passed for a 107-Room Home2 Suites Hotel",
      brief: "A Washington appeals court held that Oak Harbor's ordinance easing the sale of park land for a 107-room Home2 Suites hotel parking lot needed environmental review under SEPA.",
      category: "zoning-land-use",
      status: "ruling",
      date: "2026-10-19",
      jurisdiction: "Washington Court of Appeals, Division One",
      state: "WA",
      amount: "Declaratory relief (SEPA review of Ordinance 1999)",
      source: "live",
      sourceUrl: "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/11038320/whidbey-enviromental-action-network-v-city-of-oak-harbor/",
      propertyType: "Hospitality",
      summary: "On October 19, 2026, Washington's Court of Appeals, Division One, reversed a summary judgment for the City of Oak Harbor and revived an environmental group's challenge to Ordinance 1999. The ordinance dropped the voter-approval requirement for selling developed park land when the city approves a qualifying development agreement. The city adopted it to accommodate Sound Development Group LLC's proposed four-story, 107-room Home2 Suites by Hilton hotel, whose parking lot would take part of Hal Ramaley Memorial Park. The court held the ordinance is a nonproject action that is not categorically exempt from State Environmental Policy Act (SEPA) review.",
      significance: "Developers whose projects depend on a city changing its code, especially to free up public land, should expect that legislation to need its own environmental review under SEPA. The ruling says review must happen at the earliest stage, so an ordinance that puts conditions on how land will be used is not exempt just because it reads as procedural. The opinion is unpublished, but it shows how Washington courts may treat project-driven code amendments.",
      body: [
        "In September 2023, Sound Development Group LLC proposed a commercial and residential project in Oak Harbor on land next to Hal Ramaley Memorial Park: a one-story brewpub with single-story residential units. The city rejected that plan and encouraged a proposal with more urban density for downtown. The developer came back with a four-story Home2 Suites by Hilton with 107 rooms and meeting space. Its parking lot would extend onto part of the park.",
        "At the time, the city code required voters to approve any sale of developed park property. According to the opinion, city planning staff gave the developer several options: hold an election, drop the fourth floor, cut the pool and other amenities to free up parking, or amend the code. The developer pursued the amendment. At an August 13, 2024 public hearing, the city council passed Ordinance 1999 by a six-to-one vote. It allows park property to be sold or traded without a vote if the council approves a development agreement and finds that the exchange meets set land-area and market-value tests, or that the private party's park improvements are worth at least 150 percent of the land conveyed. The city's planner told the council the change was prompted by the hotel proposal.",
        "Whidbey Environmental Action Network sued on August 30, 2024. It argued the city violated SEPA by passing the ordinance without first reviewing its environmental impacts. The trial court granted summary judgment to the city on May 1, 2025, ruling that the ordinance was not an action covered by SEPA and was categorically exempt in any event, and dismissed the suit with prejudice.",
        "Division One reversed in an unpublished opinion. Writing for the panel, Acting Chief Judge Bowman held that Ordinance 1999 is a nonproject action under WAC 197-11-704(2)(b)(i). Its conditions tie the transfer of park land to a specific purpose, development, and so they control the use or modification of the environment, much as a zoning ordinance does. The court rejected the city's position that review could wait until a sale or development was actually proposed. It noted that a concrete proposal already existed, with site plans, floor plans, landscaping plans and renderings showing the parking lot, and that accommodating the project was the city's motivation for the ordinance. The court also held that the procedural-action exemption in WAC 197-11-800(19) does not apply, because the ordinance contains substantive standards. The case goes back to the trial court for further proceedings."
      ],
      tags: ["sepa", "hotel development", "park land", "oak harbor", "washington"],
      parties: [
        { name: "Whidbey Environmental Action Network", role: "Plaintiff/Appellant" },
        { name: "City of Oak Harbor", role: "Defendant/Respondent" }
      ],
      judge: "Acting Chief Judge Bowman",
